>>
>> How does Lojban distinguishthe following?
>> "I know who she knows that he likes"=3D "I know that(WH)x she knows that=
 he
>> likes x"
>> "I know that she knows who he likes"? =3D "I know that she knows that(WH=
)x he
>> likes x"
>> -- these differ wrt the scope of the WH complementizer.
>
> I would say they are:
>
>   mi djuno lo du'u ma kau goi ko'a zo'u ko'e djuno lo du'u ko'i nelci ko'=
a
>
>   mi djuno lo du'u ko'e djuno lo du'u ko'i nelci ma kau
>
> The second one being equivalent to expanded form:
>
>   mi djuno lo du'u ko'e djuno lo du'u ma kau goi ko'a zo'u ko'i nelci ko'=
a

These are the obvious ways, indeed. They require two rules, one that says "=
lo du'u Q kau zo'u" expresses -- probably idiomatically rather than composi=
tionally -- an interrogativity "complementizer" (or whatever you want to ca=
ll it), and another that says that an unprenexed Q-kau should be construed =
as belonging to the innermost prenex. The latter rule is doubtless redundan=
t to a more general rule that unprenexed stuff, such as quantifiers, belong=
 to the innermost prenex.

And ideally an additional rule that translates "Q(+pau)" (i.e. kau-less Q-w=
ords) into "illocution lo du'u Q kau zo'u", too.

The satisfactoriness of "lo du'u Q kau zo'u" might depend on whether idioma=
ticity is compatible with Lojban principles. If not compatible, then a new =
construction is called for.

